WEBVTT

1
00:00:12.779 --> 00:00:18.561
the dancers made their way to the ballet foyer, they discussed the horrible death of Buquet.

2
00:00:19.661 --> 00:00:26.323
On the stairs Mercier, the managers' assistant, stopped them when he overheard their conversation.

3
00:00:26.343 --> 00:00:29.244
"Have you girls heard about Buquet already?

4
00:00:30.560 --> 00:00:33.101
Most people haven't, so please forget about it for tonight."

5
00:00:33.821 --> 00:00:35.422
He waggled a finger at them.

6
00:00:35.442 --> 00:00:39.324
"And don't let Monsieur Debienne and Monsieur Poligny hear.

7
00:00:40.484 --> 00:00:43.205
It would upset the managers too much on their last night at the opera house."

8
00:00:44.246 --> 00:00:48.648
By the time the dancers reached the foyer, it was already full of people.

9
00:00:49.728 --> 00:00:53.770
Everyone was talking about the amazing performance they'd just witnessed.

10
00:00:55.310 --> 00:01:00.577
Many of the great composers of the day had appeared onstage, conducting their own works.

11
00:01:01.518 --> 00:01:06.302
But the real star was the young singer Christine Daaé.

12
00:01:08.524 --> 00:01:14.849
She'd begun with a few selections from Romeo and Juliet and concluded by singing the leading role of

13
00:01:14.929 --> 00:01:17.631
Marguerite in some excerpts from Faust.

14
00:01:19.012 --> 00:01:25.938
Carlotta, a famous Spanish diva, usually sang Marguerite, but she was ill that night.

15
00:01:27.179 --> 00:01:32.129
So Christine, who usually portrayed a boy in Faust, had taken her place.

16
00:01:33.530 --> 00:01:36.731
And what a magnificent performance she'd given!

17
00:01:39.071 --> 00:01:46.674
The whole audience had risen to its feet, shouting, cheering, clapping, while a sobbing Christine was overcome by

18
00:01:46.714 --> 00:01:47.394
the response.

19
00:01:48.895 --> 00:01:53.236
She'd fainted in the arms of other singers and been carried to her dressing room.

20
00:01:55.224 --> 00:02:01.307
Everyone in the foyer wanted to know why Christine hadn't appeared in a leading role before this performance.

21
00:02:02.268 --> 00:02:07.010
Even more mysterious was the rumor that she didn't have a voice coach.

22
00:02:07.030 --> 00:02:14.133
"I hear she plans to keep rehearsing on her own with no intention of finding a coach," one

23
00:02:14.213 --> 00:02:16.054
audience member confided to another.

24
00:02:16.074 --> 00:02:19.196
"Well, whatever she's doing, it's working.

25
00:02:20.176 --> 00:02:27.118
I attended one of her performances six months ago, and the difference between then and now The other

26
00:02:27.138 --> 00:02:27.998
man paused.

27
00:02:28.018 --> 00:02:30.339
"It's unbelievable."

28
00:02:31.359 --> 00:02:38.881
Philippe, the Count de Chagny, should have been in the foyer, supporting his favorite dancer, Sorelli, as she

29
00:02:38.921 --> 00:02:40.482
gave her speech to the managers.

30
00:02:41.722 --> 00:02:50.205
Instead he was following his much younger brother, Raoul, the Viscount de Chagny, through the maze of passages

31
00:02:50.285 --> 00:02:52.207
that led to Christine's dressing room.

32
00:02:52.227 --> 00:02:55.269
"Why are you in such a hurry?"

33
00:02:55.689 --> 00:02:56.870
Philippe asked his brother.

34
00:02:56.890 --> 00:02:59.172
"Didn't you see that poor woman faint?

35
00:02:59.772 --> 00:03:01.133
I must make sure she's all right!"

36
00:03:01.974 --> 00:03:05.096
Philippe smiled at his younger brother's urgent tone.

37
00:03:05.916 --> 00:03:11.480
Raoul was only 21, and obviously infatuated with the lovely young Christine.

38
00:03:12.321 --> 00:03:18.557
The two brothers forced their way through a crowd of singers and fans gathered outside Christine's dressing room.

39
00:03:20.718 --> 00:03:24.139
They reached her door just as the theater's doctor was arriving.

40
00:03:25.260 --> 00:03:30.722
Raoul rushed to the unconscious singer's side while Philippe and the others pushed into the room.

41
00:03:30.742 --> 00:03:35.144
"Everyone should clear the room," Raoul said to the doctor.

42
00:03:35.164 --> 00:03:37.445
"There's no space to breathe in here."

43
00:03:37.465 --> 00:03:39.466
"You're quite right."

44
00:03:40.286 --> 00:03:44.908
The doctor sent everyone away except Christine's maid and Raoul.

45
00:03:47.564 --> 00:03:51.066
Christine's eyelashes fluttered as she regained consciousness.

46
00:03:52.047 --> 00:03:55.969
Turning her head, she spotted Raoul at her side and started.

47
00:03:55.989 --> 00:04:02.973
"Monsieur," she said in a barely audible voice, "who are you?"

48
00:04:02.993 --> 00:04:09.217
"Mademoiselle Raoul knelt on one knee and kissed her hand.

49
00:04:09.237 --> 00:04:13.820
"I'm the boy who went into the sea to rescue your scarf."

50
00:04:14.764 --> 00:04:20.868
Christine looked nervously at the doctor and her maid, trying to gauge what they thought of Raoul's odd

51
00:04:20.928 --> 00:04:28.112
remark. When they laughed, Christine joined in, though she still looked uneasy.

52
00:04:30.353 --> 00:04:33.455
Raoul turned red with embarrassment and stood up.

53
00:04:35.055 --> 00:04:40.378
"Mademoiselle, since you don't seem to recognize me, I'd like to say something to you in private.

54
00:04:41.459 --> 00:04:42.940
Something very important."

55
00:04:44.192 --> 00:04:45.553
Christine's voice shook.

56
00:04:46.634 --> 00:04:48.355
"You must wait until I am better."

57
00:04:49.196 --> 00:04:50.176
The doctor nodded.

58
00:04:50.197 --> 00:04:53.119
"Please go now and let me attend to mademoiselle."

59
00:04:53.139 --> 00:04:57.302
"Thank you, doctor, but I would like to be alone."

60
00:04:58.302 --> 00:05:00.504
Christine looked nervously around the room.

61
00:05:00.524 --> 00:05:03.466
"Please go away, all of you."

62
00:05:04.647 --> 00:05:11.072
The doctor tried to protest, but observing Christine's agitation, he escorted Raoul from the room.

63
00:05:13.496 --> 00:05:18.120
"She's not her usual self; she's usually so gentle," the doctor said.

64
00:05:18.881 --> 00:05:23.004
He bid Raoul good night and left him in the now deserted passage.

65
00:05:24.945 --> 00:05:31.351
Raoul thought Christine might attend the farewell ceremony in the ballet foyer, so he lingered in the shadows

66
00:05:31.431 --> 00:05:32.251
near her doorway.

67
00:05:33.713 --> 00:05:39.037
Soon the dressing room door opened, and her maid came out, carrying a bundle of laundry.

68
00:05:39.057 --> 00:05:42.440
"Excuse me, how is she?"

69
00:05:42.888 --> 00:05:43.748
Raoul asked.

70
00:05:44.689 --> 00:05:48.049
"She's quite well, but you must not disturb her because she wants to be alone.

71
00:05:48.450 --> 00:05:49.050
Good night, sir."

72
00:05:50.710 --> 00:05:51.850
The maid hurried away.

73
00:05:51.870 --> 00:05:57.672
"Of course Christine told her maid that she wants to be alone," Raoul said to himself.

74
00:05:57.692 --> 00:06:00.553
"That's because she's waiting for me.

75
00:06:01.613 --> 00:06:03.854
She knows I want to speak to her in private."

76
00:06:04.954 --> 00:06:10.115
Raoul was about to knock on the door when he heard a man's voice inside the room.

77
00:06:10.135 --> 00:06:13.665
"Christine, do you love me?"

78
00:06:14.185 --> 00:06:14.926
the man said.

79
00:06:14.946 --> 00:06:19.729
"How can you ask that when I sing only for you?"

80
00:06:20.129 --> 00:06:23.572
Christine sounded sad and on the verge of tears.

81
00:06:25.033 --> 00:06:30.597
Raoul's heart was pounding so loudly that he was sure Christine and the man could hear it.

82
00:06:32.778 --> 00:06:33.879
The man spoke again.

83
00:06:33.899 --> 00:06:36.961
"Are you very tired, my dear?"

84
00:06:36.981 --> 00:06:43.000
"Yes, I gave you my soul tonight, and I'm dead tired."

85
00:06:44.301 --> 00:06:47.782
"Your soul is a beautiful thing, and I thank you.

86
00:06:49.462 --> 00:06:52.523
No emperor ever received so rare a gift."

87
00:06:53.763 --> 00:06:57.384
Overcome with jealousy, Raoul heard nothing after that.

88
00:06:58.205 --> 00:07:03.286
But he now understood why Christine was so nervous: she was expecting this visitor.

89
00:07:04.526 --> 00:07:09.288
Raoul returned to the shadows, determined to wait for the man to leave the room.

90
00:07:12.088 --> 00:07:16.770
To his surprise, Christine appeared first, and she was alone.

91
00:07:17.970 --> 00:07:21.392
Raoul was still too rattled by what he'd overheard to approach her.

92
00:07:22.772 --> 00:07:28.155
He waited a few minutes before opening the door to her dressing room and found himself in complete

93
00:07:28.275 --> 00:07:31.256
darkness because the gaslights had been turned off.

94
00:07:32.796 --> 00:07:36.798
"I know someone's in here," Raoul said with his back against the door.

95
00:07:36.818 --> 00:07:38.879
"Why are you hiding?"

96
00:07:40.260 --> 00:07:46.574
He lit the gas and searched behind the curtain that divided the room, but no one was there.

97
00:07:46.594 --> 00:07:48.975
"I must be going mad!"

98
00:07:49.375 --> 00:07:49.795
he thought.

99
00:07:50.616 --> 00:07:52.556
Raoul left the room in a daze.

100
00:07:53.537 --> 00:07:56.918
At the bottom of a staircase he felt an icy draft.

101
00:07:58.199 --> 00:08:03.021
Suddenly two men were behind him, carrying something under a white sheet.

102
00:08:03.041 --> 00:08:06.322
"Excuse me," Raoul said.

103
00:08:06.342 --> 00:08:08.623
"Can you tell me the way out?"

104
00:08:09.523 --> 00:08:12.612
"The door's open straight ahead," one man grunted.

105
00:08:12.632 --> 00:08:14.253
"Thank you.

106
00:08:14.913 --> 00:08:16.013
What's that you're carrying?"

107
00:08:16.033 --> 00:08:23.015
"That is Joseph Buquet, the chief stagehand, whose body was found tonight in the third cellar beneath the

108
00:08:23.095 --> 00:08:25.216
stage," the man explained.

109
00:08:26.496 --> 00:08:32.438
Raoul removed his hat as a mark of respect for the dead and let the men pass.
